Under RH 4.1, you can now get the X TGA server to install automatically.
The only thing I had to do was edit the config file (step 4 below) to set
up for my monitor.  It worked great!

In <1.5.4.32.19970401041719.00686718@knuth.mtsu.edu>, on 03/31/97 
   at 10:17 PM, ripp0022@knuth.mtsu.edu said:

>At 11:29 AM 3/31/97 -0600, you wrote:
>>     I have a Multia Dec alpha running Linux 4.0 for alphas.   I am 
>>trying to set up XWindows on it.  Can anyone tell me which video card 
>>I need to select for it?  I have not been successful with it yet :(
>>
>>Thanks in advance,
>>
>>
>>Peter Barton
>>Network Administrator
>>FlashNet Communications
>>peter@flash.net
>>
>>--
>>To unsubscribe: send e-mail to axp-list-request@redhat.com with
>>'unsubscribe' as the subject.  Do not send it to axp-list@redhat.com
>>

>Peter, 
>        I have the same box and have had to do the by-hand install. Its
>fairly easy to do, just follow these steps... (If there is a better way
>yet, please let me know...)

>1. Mount your RH CD, and navigate to the /X directory
>2. Copy the axpbin-ELF-EVx-test.gz and the alpha-Xtga-BETA-0.0.README to
>your home dir. 
>3. Gzip -d the *.gz file, this is the X server, then copy (or mv) it to
>this file name...XF86_TGA.
>4. Then, edit the *.README file (w/ vi or something), and delete the
>first part of the file until you get to the line "# this file
>generated........." then save this file as "XF86Config" in you home dir.
>5. OK, your almost there... copy the newly named XF86_TGA to
>/usr/X11R6/bin 6. Create a symbolic link from the server to X thusly:
>rm /usr/X11R6/bin/X <cr>
>ln -s /usr/X11R6/bin/XF86_TGA /usr/X11R6/bin/X
>7. Chmod the /usr/X11R6/bin/XF86_TGA file so you can use it. 8. Copy the
>XF86Config file from your home dir to /etc/X11/XF86Config (yes overwrite
>the old one)
>9. startx

>Viola, you've got an Xwindows system. (unless you or I goofed somewhere)
>Give this a try and you should be up and running. If not, take 20 paces
>and draw!

>Good luck, Tiger

