From jestabro@amt.tay1.dec.com Mon Apr 21 22:16:39 1997
Message-ID: <199704220207.WAA01034@alpha0.estabrook.org>
Date: Mon, 21 Apr 1997 22:07:15 +0500
From: jestabro@amt.tay1.dec.com <jestabro@amt.tay1.dec.com>
Subject: Re: Dead TGA Video
In-Reply-To: <199704220000.KAA113644@aupozx668.btal.btco.com>

>>> Jason Ball said:
>
> Do other people have problems using the onboard video adapter with the SRM
> console ?

It's more likely a problem with the monitor you have hooked up, than with
SRM, as the UDB *always* boots to ARC via SRM, as those with multi-sync
monitors may have already noticed... :-)

The SRM console reads some jumpers on the UDB MB which indicate the "default"
resolution/refresh rate for the TGA to produce. Most have been setup for
1024x768 at 70Hz or thereabouts, but could be set as high as 1280x1024@70Hz,
or as low as 640x480@60Hz.

Many people, on power up, are first presented with a black screen and small
characters for a few lines, then, as SRM transfers control to ARC, the
familiar blue screen comes up with large white characters.  ARC does its own
thing, ignoring the jumpers, and sets 640x480@60Hz, which is pretty much why
the LINUX TGA console code does the same.

If your monitor is incapable of the SRM "default" setting, you may never
see the SRM-related messages.

If you wish SRM to use a setting that's more in line with the monitor's
capabilities, refer to some of the UDB-related webpages out there for the
possible jumper configurations, or search the axp-list archives, as I know
it has appeared there.

--Jay++

